Port of the upstream rtl8188eus (v5.3.9) join-abort mechanism into the old
in-kernel Rockchip rtl8188eu driver (v5.2.2.3_24783, 2017).

Problem this closes on RG351MP mesh roaming:
  cfg80211_rtw_disconnect() aborts a running scan (rtw_scan_abort) but does NOT
  wait for a join/association that may still be in flight. If wpa_supplicant's
  association timeout fires while the driver is stuck in _FW_UNDER_LINKING (join
  frame sent, AP not yet answering), the disconnect path runs
  free_assoc_resources()+indicate_disconnect() immediately while the join is
  still live in the cmd queue. A late report_join_res then sets _FW_LINKED after
  the disconnect already happened -> SME/fw_state divergence.

Mechanism (as in official v5.3.9):
  rtw_join_abort_timeout() nudges the next MLME cycle (set_link_timer 1ms) and
  then blocks the disconnect path for up to timeout_ms, polling until
  _FW_UNDER_LINKING clears. This turns the disconnect into a barrier: a pending
  join is completed/aborted before the disconnect tears down the association.
  The join_abort flag itself is only bookkeeping/logging in this driver (there
  is no if(join_abort) check in link_timer_hdl); the real effect is the
  set_link_timer nudge plus the wait loop.

Adaptations vs. the official source (old driver has no systime type and no
cfg80211 connect-request tracking):
  * use "u32 start" (this driver's convention) instead of "systime start"
  * drop the "|| rtw_cfg80211_is_connect_requested(adapter)" clauses
    (rtw_cfg80211_is_connect_requested does not exist in v5.2.2.3)

All primitives used already exist: set_link_timer (rtw_mlme_ext.h),
check_fwstate/_FW_UNDER_LINKING, rtw_get_current_time/rtw_get_passing_time_ms,
rtw_msleep_os, RTW_CANNOT_RUN, FUNC_ADPT_FMT/ARG.

